1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,121
A hot spring, steaming.

2
00:00:02,121 --> 00:00:04,147
A salt pan, white with salt.

3
00:00:04,147 --> 00:00:05,683
The stomach of a cow.

4
00:00:06,083 --> 00:00:07,569
Three harsh places.

5
00:00:07,569 --> 00:00:10,724
Yet tiny microbes live
happily in all three.

6
00:00:11,174 --> 00:00:14,289
On a cycling trip, Aarav
stopped at a hot spring.

7
00:00:14,289 --> 00:00:16,982
A sign said bacteria
live in the hot water.

8
00:00:16,982 --> 00:00:21,049
He asked: are they the same kind
of life as the bacteria in curd?

9
00:00:21,399 --> 00:00:23,715
Under a microscope, they look alike.

10
00:00:23,715 --> 00:00:26,032
But inside, they are very different.

11
00:00:26,032 --> 00:00:30,452
Today you see how one scientist split
all life into three big groups, called

12
00:00:30,452 --> 00:00:31,352
domains.

13
00:00:31,758 --> 00:00:33,283
First, where we start from.

14
00:00:33,633 --> 00:00:38,067
In the five kingdom system, all
organisms without a nucleus sit together

15
00:00:38,067 --> 00:00:39,794
in one kingdom, Monera.

16
00:00:40,244 --> 00:00:43,326
A cell without a true
nucleus is a prokaryote.

17
00:00:43,326 --> 00:00:45,540
Its DNA lies loose in the cell.

18
00:00:45,540 --> 00:00:48,113
A cell with a nucleus is a eukaryote.

19
00:00:48,563 --> 00:00:53,164
The other four kingdoms, Protista,
Fungi, Plantae and Animalia, are all

20
00:00:53,164 --> 00:00:54,064
eukaryotes.

21
00:00:54,013 --> 00:00:56,600
So Monera looked like one simple group.

22
00:00:57,250 --> 00:01:01,824
Then a scientist named Carl Woese looked
deeper, at one part found in every

23
00:01:01,824 --> 00:01:02,724
cell.

24
00:01:02,715 --> 00:01:04,885
Every living cell has ribosomes.

25
00:01:04,885 --> 00:01:07,630
These are tiny parts that build proteins.

26
00:01:08,130 --> 00:01:09,042
Zoom in.

27
00:01:09,042 --> 00:01:14,526
A ribosome is made of protein and a
special RNA, called ribosomal RNA.

28
00:01:15,026 --> 00:01:16,020
Zoom again.

29
00:01:16,020 --> 00:01:20,859
Ribosomal RNA is a long chain of four
kinds of building blocks, written

30
00:01:20,859 --> 00:01:23,310
as letters: A, U, G and C.

31
00:01:23,810 --> 00:01:27,573
All cells need ribosomes, so
all cells carry this molecule.

32
00:01:27,573 --> 00:01:31,307
That makes it a fair way to
compare any two living things.

33
00:01:31,957 --> 00:01:34,228
Now watch how the comparison works.

34
00:01:34,578 --> 00:01:35,546
Step one.

35
00:01:35,546 --> 00:01:40,465
Read the order of letters in the
ribosomal RNA of each organism.

36
00:01:41,215 --> 00:01:42,115
Step two.

37
00:01:41,997 --> 00:01:45,283
Line the chains up side
by side, letter by letter.

38
00:01:46,033 --> 00:01:46,954
Step three.

39
00:01:46,954 --> 00:01:49,728
Count the places where the letters differ.

40
00:01:49,728 --> 00:01:52,130
Few differences mean close relatives.

41
00:01:52,130 --> 00:01:54,433
Many differences mean distant ones.

42
00:01:55,183 --> 00:01:56,083
Step four.

43
00:01:56,049 --> 00:02:00,151
Join the closest ones first,
and a family tree of life grows.

44
00:02:00,901 --> 00:02:05,442
Woese found that the microbes from
harsh places differed from ordinary

45
00:02:05,442 --> 00:02:08,603
bacteria as much as
bacteria differ from us.

46
00:02:09,253 --> 00:02:13,769
So in nineteen ninety, Woese and his
co-workers proposed three domains.

47
00:02:14,119 --> 00:02:16,350
A domain is a rank above kingdom.

48
00:02:16,350 --> 00:02:18,486
It is the biggest group of all.

49
00:02:18,936 --> 00:02:23,687
Domain Bacteria holds the true bacteria,
like the ones that turn milk into

50
00:02:23,687 --> 00:02:24,587
curd.

51
00:02:24,712 --> 00:02:29,939
Domain Archaea holds the microbes of
harsh places: salty water, hot springs,

52
00:02:29,939 --> 00:02:30,980
and marshes.

53
00:02:31,430 --> 00:02:34,848
Domain Eukarya holds every
organism with a nucleus.

54
00:02:34,848 --> 00:02:38,370
Protists, fungi, plants
and animals all sit here.

55
00:02:39,019 --> 00:02:41,898
Now compare the two
domains without a nucleus.

56
00:02:42,248 --> 00:02:43,148
Bacteria.

57
00:02:43,070 --> 00:02:44,064
No nucleus.

58
00:02:44,064 --> 00:02:48,561
Most have a cell wall built with
a material called peptidoglycan.

59
00:02:48,561 --> 00:02:50,658
Example: the curd bacteria.

60
00:02:51,108 --> 00:02:52,008
Archaea.

61
00:02:51,852 --> 00:02:53,139
Also no nucleus.

62
00:02:53,139 --> 00:02:58,248
But their cell wall is different, and
this helps them survive harsh places.

63
00:02:58,248 --> 00:03:03,619
Example: methanogens, which make methane
gas in the gut of cows and buffaloes.

64
00:03:04,069 --> 00:03:04,992
Your turn.

65
00:03:04,992 --> 00:03:09,759
A microbe lives in a salt lake,
in water far saltier than the sea.

66
00:03:09,759 --> 00:03:11,277
Bacteria or Archaea?

67
00:03:11,277 --> 00:03:12,636
Pause and decide.

68
00:03:15,836 --> 00:03:16,736
Archaea!

69
00:03:16,620 --> 00:03:21,546
Microbes of extremely salty places
are called halophiles, and they belong

70
00:03:21,546 --> 00:03:22,562
to Archaea.

71
00:03:23,213 --> 00:03:24,176
Remember this.

72
00:03:24,176 --> 00:03:27,362
Three domains: two without
a nucleus, and one with.

73
00:03:27,962 --> 00:03:29,704
Now, a worked example.

74
00:03:29,704 --> 00:03:32,879
Let's sort six organisms
into the three domains.

75
00:03:33,229 --> 00:03:37,603
The curd bacterium has no nucleus
and a normal bacterial wall.

76
00:03:37,603 --> 00:03:38,879
Domain Bacteria.

77
00:03:39,329 --> 00:03:41,351
A methanogen in a cow's gut.

78
00:03:41,351 --> 00:03:43,518
No nucleus, a different wall.

79
00:03:43,518 --> 00:03:44,674
Domain Archaea.

80
00:03:44,674 --> 00:03:47,182
So is a halophile from a salt pan.

81
00:03:47,632 --> 00:03:49,198
Yeast has a nucleus.

82
00:03:49,198 --> 00:03:50,374
Domain Eukarya.

83
00:03:50,374 --> 00:03:53,033
So do the amoeba and the mango tree.

84
00:03:53,483 --> 00:03:56,637
Notice the old kingdom
Monera has split in two.

85
00:03:56,637 --> 00:03:59,931
Bacteria and Archaea now
stand in separate domains.

86
00:04:00,581 --> 00:04:02,046
Now, the exam trap.

87
00:04:02,397 --> 00:04:06,436
Many students think Archaea are
just one more type of bacteria.

88
00:04:06,836 --> 00:04:09,253
But Archaea are a separate domain.

89
00:04:09,253 --> 00:04:14,254
By their ribosomal RNA, they are even
closer to Eukarya than to Bacteria.

90
00:04:14,904 --> 00:04:16,438
Where is this asked?

91
00:04:16,438 --> 00:04:19,465
This part is not in
the current NCERT text.

92
00:04:19,465 --> 00:04:24,232
If it comes up, expect a statement
question on who proposed the domains,

93
00:04:24,232 --> 00:04:26,929
or which domain an organism belongs to.

94
00:04:27,529 --> 00:04:28,747
Exam level now.

95
00:04:28,747 --> 00:04:30,543
Read the four statements.

96
00:04:30,543 --> 00:04:32,236
Which ones are correct?

97
00:04:32,236 --> 00:04:33,506
Pause and solve.

98
00:04:38,906 --> 00:04:40,196
The answer is D.

99
00:04:40,196 --> 00:04:42,295
Statements one, two and four.

100
00:04:42,795 --> 00:04:43,927
Three is wrong.

101
00:04:43,927 --> 00:04:47,918
Fungi have a nucleus, so they
belong to Eukarya, not Archaea.

102
00:04:48,418 --> 00:04:50,516
One, two and four are true.

103
00:04:50,516 --> 00:04:52,227
Woese, nineteen ninety.

104
00:04:52,227 --> 00:04:54,213
A domain sits above kingdom.

105
00:04:54,213 --> 00:04:56,627
And it was built on ribosomal RNA.

106
00:04:57,227 --> 00:04:58,685
Here is your recap.

107
00:04:58,685 --> 00:05:00,185
Screenshot this card.

108
00:05:03,385 --> 00:05:06,221
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

109
00:05:06,621 --> 00:05:07,521
One.

110
00:05:07,157 --> 00:05:09,428
Name the three domains of life.

111
00:05:10,328 --> 00:05:11,228
Two.

112
00:05:10,887 --> 00:05:15,565
Why did Woese compare ribosomal
RNA, and not the shape of cells?

113
00:05:16,465 --> 00:05:17,365
Three.

114
00:05:17,055 --> 00:05:20,036
Which old kingdom was
split across two domains?

115
00:05:21,136 --> 00:05:25,070
Next time: a mushroom grows
from the soil like a plant.

116
00:05:25,070 --> 00:05:27,079
So why is it not a plant?

117
00:05:27,079 --> 00:05:30,730
We find out in the basis of
biological classification.
